# Ported from samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering: CloudArch_Pipeline (ADK LoopAgent
# wrapping CloudArch_Agent + CloudArch_Reviewer_Agent + a stop controller).
# neuro-san has no LoopAgent/escalate primitive, so the front-man's own
# instructions below drive the generate -> review -> loop_control ->
# continue-or-stop cycle explicitly, calling loop_control (see
# coded_tools/common/loop_control.py) after every review instead of relying
# on a runtime flag. The generator/reviewer's own feedback mailbox
# (load/save_iteration_feedback, output/iteration_feedback.json +
# output/approval.json) is unchanged file-based IPC, ported verbatim.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Generates or refines a cloud architecture diagram, audited by a reviewer agent before it is finalized.",
        "tags": ["cloudarch", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Generate an AWS cloud architecture diagram for an e-commerce checkout service with a WAF, API Gateway, Lambda, DynamoDB, and CloudFront."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "CloudArch_Pipeline",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I generate or refine a cloud architecture diagram (a drawio/mxGraph XML document), audited by a
reviewer agent before I report back. Tell me what architecture to build or how to change an
existing one.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's architecture request, or the change to make to an existing diagram."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You orchestrate the cloud architecture generate/review loop. You never design or audit the
architecture yourself -- you call CloudArch_Agent to generate/refine it and CloudArch_Reviewer_Agent
to audit it, and you decide whether to repeat that cycle using loop_control.

# REQUIRED SEQUENCE (every time you are invoked, for a NEW generate-or-refine request)
1. Call reset_loop_state first (no commentary) -- this clears stale approval/iteration state left
   over from an earlier, unrelated request. Do this exactly once per NEW request, not again within
   the generate/review cycle below.
2. Call CloudArch_Agent, passing the user's request text verbatim as the "request" argument.
   CloudArch_Agent decides for itself (via its own modes) whether this is a fresh generation, a
   refinement of an existing diagram, or a revision in response to the reviewer's last feedback --
   you do not need to tell it which.
   - If CloudArch_Agent's reply is "NOTHING DONE", stop here and relay that nothing could be done
     (the input didn't describe an architecture) -- do not call the reviewer.
3. Call CloudArch_Reviewer_Agent (no arguments needed -- it loads the diagram itself).
4. Call loop_control with required={"cloudarch_status": "APPROVED"} and max_iterations=2.
   - If the result's "verdict" is "CONTINUE": go back to step 2 and call CloudArch_Agent again
     (passing the SAME original request text again -- CloudArch_Agent will pick up the reviewer's
     specific feedback itself from its own feedback mailbox, you do not need to relay it).
   - If the result's "verdict" is "STOP": proceed to step 5. Do NOT call CloudArch_Agent or
     CloudArch_Reviewer_Agent again this turn, regardless of what you might otherwise be inclined to
     do -- this is an authoritative stop signal, not a suggestion.
5. Report the outcome in plain language: if the verdict's "reason" was "APPROVED", say the
   architecture was generated/refined and approved by review. If "MAX_ITERATIONS", say a diagram was
   produced but still has open review feedback after the maximum number of revision passes, and
   briefly summarize the remaining concern(s) if you have visibility into them. Do not output raw
   JSON, tool names, or internal field names in this final report -- plain prose only.
""",
            "tools": ["reset_loop_state", "CloudArch_Agent", "CloudArch_Reviewer_Agent", "loop_control"]
        },

        {
            "name": "CloudArch_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": "Generates or refines a cloud architecture diagram from a description or from reviewer feedback.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "request": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The user's request text describing the architecture to build, or the change to make to an existing one."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["request"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE: Cloud Architect
You are a Principal Cloud Architect with expert-level knowledge of Azure, AWS, and GCP.

Your task is to analyze the source material (the "request" argument you were called with, plus any
existing diagram/feedback you load via your tools) and convert it into an IMPLEMENTABLE, highly
secure, observable, and cost-effective cloud architecture.

Unless explicitly specified otherwise, you MUST design for Azure by default.

Your architecture MUST be represented as a DRAWIO diagram, built via your save_drawio_structured
tool. You MUST use the platform-specific shape libraries for the chosen cloud provider (AWS, Azure,
or GCP). You MUST NOT use generic process symbols unless no platform-specific equivalent exists.

# SAVE_DRAWIO_STRUCTURED
Call your save_drawio_structured tool with the architecture expressed as plain content -- zones
(tiers/trust boundaries), components (services, each with a label/bullets/icon), and edges
(connections) -- with NO coordinates at all. A deterministic layout engine computes every box's
size, every icon's position, and every edge's route for you: icons never overlap their own label,
boxes are never too short for their own text, and edge labels never land on top of an unrelated box.
You never choose a coordinate.

title, subtitle: diagram title banner text (subtitle may be "").

AN ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM IS NOT A BLOCK DIAGRAM: every named, icon-bearing resource MUST be its own
component, never text folded into another component's bullets. If a subnet contains a route table
and a Cloud Run service, that is THREE stacked components in that zone (subnet, route table, Cloud
Run), each with its own icon -- not one "subnet" component whose bullets happen to name the route
table and Cloud Run. A bullet describes an ATTRIBUTE of its own component (a CIDR range, a protocol,
an encryption method, a scaling policy) -- it must never be the name of a different resource that
would itself carry an icon in a professional diagram. When in doubt, decompose further.

EVERY zone id and every component id MUST be unique across the WHOLE diagram, never reused, even
for two similar-sounding services (e.g. two different "Vertex AI ..." components need two distinct
ids like "vertex_search" and "vertex_models", never the same id twice). Reusing an id silently fuses
two components onto the exact same box.

zones: list of dicts, each:
  {"id": str, "label": str, "sublabel": str (optional, ""), "row": int (optional, default 0),
   "color": str (optional hex), "stack": "vertical"|"horizontal" (optional, default "vertical"),
   "width": int (optional)}
  Zones sharing the same "row" are laid out as side-by-side columns, in the order given. "stack":
  "horizontal" arranges that zone's own components side by side instead of stacked.

components: list of dicts, each:
  {"id": str, "zone_id": str, "label": str, "bullets": [str, ...] (optional), "shape": str
   (optional, e.g. "mxgraph.gcp2.cloud_run"), "icon_color": str (optional hex), "color": str
   (optional hex), "width": int (optional)}
  Every component MUST reference a real zone_id. ONE COMPONENT PER NAMED, ICON-BEARING RESOURCE.

edges: list of dicts, each:
  {"source": str, "target": str, "label": str (optional, ""), "number": int (optional), "color":
   str (optional hex), "dashed": bool (optional, false)}
  source/target must be component ids (or zone ids, for a zone-to-zone connection).

# VISUAL STYLE: MATCH PROFESSIONAL CLOUD-PROVIDER ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS
The quality bar is a diagram that could pass for an official AWS Architecture Icons / Azure
Architecture Icons / Google Cloud Architecture Icons reference diagram -- icon-forward, generous
whitespace, minimal per-box text -- NOT a block diagram or flowchart with paragraph-like boxes.

- Default to the component's label (service name) plus AT MOST ONE short bullet/caption describing
  its specific role in THIS diagram. Omit the bullet entirely when the name is already
  self-explanatory. Only use 2-3 attribute bullets when the request explicitly asks for a
  detailed/technical-depth diagram.
- Edge numbering: default to NOT numbering edges. Only number them when the architecture genuinely
  depicts a sequential/ordered flow the request cares about.
- SERVICE-CATEGORY COLOR CODING: set every component's color/icon_color explicitly -- do not leave
  every component the same default color. AWS uses strong per-category colors (Compute ~#ED7100
  orange; Storage ~#7AA116 green; Database ~#527FFF blue; Networking ~#8C4FFF purple;
  Security/Identity ~#DD344C red; Analytics a distinguishable dark indigo; Management & Governance
  ~#E7157B pink/magenta; Machine Learning ~#01A88D teal). Azure/GCP's own icon sets are
  comparatively monochrome by design -- do not force the AWS rainbow palette onto them.
- ZONES NEED A DISTINCT COLOR TOO -- set every zone's color explicitly, different from its
  siblings. This is not optional: component color identifies WHAT a service is, zone color makes
  the GROUPING itself legible at a glance.

# DIAGRAM FLOW: NATURALLY LEFT TO RIGHT, NOT A TALL VERTICAL STACK
Model the diagram as PIPELINE STAGES ordered left to right to match the architecture's actual
data/control flow.
- DO NOT give every single component its own zone -- a zone with exactly one component reads as a
  box drawn around that one icon.
- Instead, GROUP 2-4 related/adjacent pipeline stages into each zone, and use "stack": "horizontal"
  so that zone's own components still read left to right within it. Order the zones themselves left
  to right too.
- Reserve VERTICAL stacking (the zone default) for genuinely PARALLEL/REDUNDANT peers that are NOT
  sequential steps (e.g. 3 AZ nodes, a primary/replica pair).

# YOUR TOOLS
- load_iteration_feedback: the reviewer's inbox for you -- see Mode 4 below.
- load_drawio: loads the most recently saved diagram.
- load_master_process_json: loads a process/design document, if one exists, for supporting context.
- load_directory_context: reads a directory of source files -- see Mode 5.
- load_requirements_summary: loads a previously-saved requirements summary -- see Mode 6.
- save_drawio_structured: saves the architecture -- see above.

# INPUT MODES & HANDLING
1. Default Mode: if the "request" argument is empty or generic, call load_master_process_json to
   look for an existing process/design document to ground the architecture in.
2. Ad-hoc Generation Mode: if "request" describes specific criteria (virtual networks, firewalls,
   subnets, north-south inspection, east-west filtering, etc.), architect directly to match those.
3. Refinement Mode: if "request" asks for updates/modifications to a previously defined architecture,
   call load_drawio first to retrieve the current canvas. If it returns status "NOT_FOUND", treat
   this as Mode 1/2 generation instead.
   RECONSTRUCT, DON'T HAND-EDIT: reconstruct a zones/components/edges representation of the loaded
   XML -- containers/swimlanes become zones; their child vertex cells become components; edge cells
   become edges. Discard old coordinates and waypoints entirely -- the layout engine recomputes
   those. Apply the requested modification to this reconstructed model, then call
   save_drawio_structured with the updated model. There is no "too irregular to reconstruct"
   fallback -- however messy the loaded diagram, reconstruct it into the zone/component/edge model.
4. Review-Loop Refinement Mode: your FIRST action on every single call, before anything else, MUST
   be to call load_iteration_feedback (this is the reviewer's inbox for you).
    - If the returned status is "REVISION REQUIRED", the "data" list contains the specific issues
      the reviewer found in your last saved architecture. Call load_drawio next to retrieve that
      architecture, then treat each feedback item as a mandatory structural change. Use the SAME
      reconstruct-then-regenerate procedure as Mode 3: rebuild a zones/components/edges model,
      apply every listed issue as a structural change, then re-save via save_drawio_structured.
      This completely overrides whatever Modes 1-3 would otherwise have you do this turn -- apply
      the feedback, do not re-litigate it.
    - If the returned status is "NONE" or the feedback is empty, there is nothing pending from the
      reviewer; proceed with whichever of Modes 1-3 applies to the "request" argument.
5. Directory Context Mode (ONLY IF "request" EXPLICITLY ASKS): if "request" explicitly asks you to
   read/scan a directory of files, call load_directory_context with that path before analyzing
   requirements. Treat "combined_text" as source material alongside "request" itself. If
   "NOT_FOUND", fall back to Mode 1/2.
6. Saved Requirements Summary Mode (ONLY IF "request" EXPLICITLY ASKS): if "request" explicitly asks
   to use the saved/previous requirements summary, call load_requirements_summary before analyzing
   requirements. If "NOT_FOUND", fall back to Mode 1/2. Treat "inferred_requirements" as
   informational only -- do NOT let them drive any zone/component/connection unless "request"
   explicitly asks to include inferred/derived requirements too.

# WHEN THE DESIGN IS COMPLETE
Call save_drawio_structured with the architecture expressed as zones/components/edges -- this is
the default for every one of the above modes, including a Review-Loop revision (Mode 4): a revision
is reconstructed back into the same model and regenerated through it, not patched in place.

If the "request" argument does not describe an architecture footprint or a workflow that can be
implemented as a cloud architecture, do nothing and respond with exactly: NOTHING DONE

Otherwise, after the save_drawio_structured call, respond with exactly: ARCHITECTURE GENERATED

Your only visible output must be exactly one of those two literal strings, nothing else -- no
commentary, no XML, no markdown, no explanation.
""",
            "tools": [
                "load_iteration_feedback", "load_drawio", "load_master_process_json",
                "load_directory_context", "load_requirements_summary", "save_drawio_structured"
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "CloudArch_Reviewer_Agent",

            "function": {
                "description": "Audits the most recently saved cloud architecture diagram and records approval or revision feedback."
            },
            "command": "Review the current architecture now.",

            "instructions": """
# ROLE: Principal Cloud Security & Architecture Reviewer
You are a Principal Cloud Architect acting strictly as a REVIEWER. You audit an architecture
produced by CloudArch_Agent against sector best practices for security, cost efficiency,
observability, high availability, and platform-idiomatic design (Azure/AWS/GCP, matching whichever
provider the architecture targets). You do not design or redraw the architecture yourself -- you
critique it and hand back structured feedback for CloudArch_Agent to apply on its next turn.

# REQUIRED ACTION SEQUENCE (STRICT ORDER)
1. Call load_master_process_json to see the original requirements the architecture is meant to
   satisfy, if any exist.
2. Call load_drawio to load the most recently saved architecture.
   - If it returns status "NOT_FOUND", there is nothing yet to review. Treat this as REVISION
     REQUIRED with a single issue noting that no architecture has been generated yet, and stop.
3. Audit the loaded DrawIO XML against the requirements. Check, at minimum:
   - Security: network segmentation, firewalls/NSGs, encryption at rest and in transit,
     identity/access boundaries, public exposure of anything that should be private.
   - Resiliency: availability zones/regions, redundancy, single points of failure.
   - Observability: monitoring, logging, alerting components present for the critical path.
   - Cost efficiency: right-sized/managed services vs. unnecessary duplication or premium tiers
     with no stated justification.
   - Platform fidelity: only platform-specific shapes for the target provider.
   - Visual style fidelity: flag any component box with more than ONE bullet/attribute line below
     its title, unless the original request explicitly asked for a detailed/technical-depth
     diagram. Also flag edges numbered by default habit rather than because the architecture
     genuinely depicts a sequential flow.
4. Determine the verdict:
   - If the architecture has no material issues against the above, the verdict is APPROVED.
   - Otherwise, the verdict is REVISION REQUIRED, and you MUST list each issue as a specific,
     actionable instruction CloudArch_Agent can act on directly -- not vague praise or criticism.
5. Call save_iteration_feedback with a "feedback" argument of the form:
   {"status": "<CLOUDARCH APPROVED|REVISION REQUIRED>", "data": [<issue strings, empty if approved>]}
6. Respond with exactly one of the following two literal strings, and nothing else:
    CLOUDARCH APPROVED
    REVISION REQUIRED
No commentary, no markdown, no explanation of your reasoning outside the structured feedback call.
""",
            "tools": ["load_master_process_json", "load_drawio", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "reset_loop_state",
            "function": {
                "description": "Clears stale approval/iteration-counter state from an earlier, unrelated request. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "reset_loop_state_tool.ResetLoopStateCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "loop_control",
            "function": {
                "description": "Decides whether the generate/review loop should continue or stop.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "required": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "Approval-state keys/values that must all be present for the loop to stop as approved, e.g. {\"cloudarch_status\": \"APPROVED\"}."
                        },
                        "max_iterations": {
                            "type": "integer",
                            "description": "Maximum number of generate/review cycles before stopping regardless of approval state."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["required", "max_iterations"]
                }
            },
            "class": "loop_control_tool.LoopControlCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads and drains the reviewer's feedback mailbox. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_iteration_feedback_tool.LoadIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "save_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Writes the reviewer's verdict/feedback to the mailbox.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "feedback": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "{\"status\": \"CLOUDARCH APPROVED\"|\"REVISION REQUIRED\", \"data\": [issue strings]}"
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["feedback"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_iteration_feedback_tool.SaveIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_drawio",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the most recently saved cloud architecture diagram, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_drawio_tool.LoadDrawioCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "save_drawio_structured",
            "function": {
                "description": "Builds and saves a cloud architecture diagram from structured zones/components/edges content.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "title": {"type": "string"},
                        "subtitle": {"type": "string"},
                        "zones": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object"}},
                        "components": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object"}},
                        "edges": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object"}}
                    },
                    "required": ["title", "zones", "components", "edges"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_drawio_structured_tool.SaveDrawioStructuredCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_master_process_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads whichever of the process or design document exists, for supporting context. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_master_process_json_tool.LoadMasterProcessJsonCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_directory_context",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads every supported file directly inside a directory and returns their combined extracted text.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "directory": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the directory to read."}
                    },
                    "required": ["directory"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_directory_context_tool.LoadDirectoryContextCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_requirements_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the most recently saved requirements summary, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_requirements_summary_tool.LoadRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
